---
import LandingLayout from '../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import ProductBlock from '../components/ProductBlock.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { page } from '../data/site';

const related = [
  page('/home-maintenance-tracker/'),
  page('/home-maintenance-records/'),
  page('/home-maintenance-app/'),
  page('/home-maintenance-calendar/'),
  page('/resources/how-to-keep-track-of-home-maintenance/')
];
---
<LandingLayout
  title="Home Maintenance Reminders for iPhone, iPad, and Mac"
  description="Set recurring home maintenance reminders and keep overdue work visible. Connect each reminder to an appliance or home system, then save the completed work in its maintenance history."
  canonicalPath="/home-maintenance-reminders/"
  breadcrumbLabel="Home maintenance reminders"
  h1="Home maintenance reminders that remember what happened"
  intro="A reminder is useful on the day a task is due. A home record is useful for years. Around The House connects the two: recurring work appears on your schedule, and completing it adds the result to that item's maintenance history."
  ctaHeading="Schedule one task that always comes back."
  ctaBody="Choose a filter change, safety check, or seasonal job and let the next due date take care of itself."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Recurring maintenance needs a complete loop</h2>
    <p>
      Many household tasks repeat every month, season, or year. Putting them on a general to-do list can produce
      an alert, but it rarely preserves the completed work or connects it to the appliance or system involved.
    </p>
    <ul class="qa-list">
      <li><strong>Choose the item</strong><span>Attach the reminder to the furnace, water heater, detector, roof, or other part of the home.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Set the date and recurrence</strong><span>Schedule it once or repeat it on the interval that fits the task.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Keep missed work visible</strong><span>An overdue task remains overdue instead of disappearing with yesterday's notification.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Complete or snooze it</strong><span>Act on the task now or move it to a more realistic date.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Save the result</strong><span>Record the work, provider, notes, cost, and photos in the item's history.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <ProductBlock
    device="iphone"
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/iphone-schedule.webp"
    alt="The Around The House maintenance reminder calendar on iPhone with a recurring task marked overdue"
  >
    <h2>See upcoming and overdue work together</h2>
    <p>
      The Schedule combines a calendar with the maintenance due on the selected day. Each reminder names the task,
      the item it belongs to, and its recurrence, so you can understand it without reconstructing the context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Missed work remains visible as overdue. You can complete it, snooze it, edit it, or remove it when it no
      longer applies.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <ProductBlock
    device="ipad"
    flip
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/ipad-today.webp"
    alt="The Today view in Around The House on iPad showing overdue work, upcoming maintenance, home areas, and items needing attention"
  >
    <h2>Start with what needs attention</h2>
    <p>
      Today summarizes overdue and upcoming work before showing the rest of the home. It is designed to answer a
      simple question: is there anything I need to deal with now?
    </p>
    <p>
      On quiet days, the answer may be no. The app does not need to invent activity to justify being opened.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <section>
    <h2>Useful reminders are specific</h2>
    <p>
      “Change filter” is less useful than a reminder connected to the furnace record containing the correct size,
      a photo of the installation direction, the last completion date, and the replacement receipt. The item
      supplies the context; the reminder supplies the timing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Private reminders across your Apple devices</h2>
    <p>
      Around The House requires no separate account. Your home information remains on your devices and, when
      iCloud synchronization is enabled, in your private iCloud. The app runs on iPhone, iPad, and Mac so the
      same schedule and history can be available where you need them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RelatedLinks heading="Related maintenance tools" items={related} />
</LandingLayout>
